#: ../../../python.rst:5
msgid "Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:7
msgid ""
"Xonsh is built on top of Python and Python code can be executed natively "
"alongside shell commands. This page collects notes on how Python-specific "
"behaviour interacts with the shell."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:12
msgid "Python versions support policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:14
msgid ""
"Xonsh adopts `NEP-0029 <https://numpy.org/neps/nep-0029-deprecation_policy."
"html>`_ in supporting Python versions. Simply speaking a minor Python "
"release (X.*) will be supported for 42 months from its date of initial "
"release. Since Python has adopted yearly release cycle, most of the time, "
"the latest 4 minor versions of Python would be supported at any given time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:23
msgid "Importing Python modules from a local directory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:25
msgid ""
"The modules available for import in a given ``xonsh`` session depend on "
"what's available in ``sys.path``. If you want to be able to import a module "
"that resides in the current directory, ensure that there is an empty string "
"as the first element of your ``sys.path``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:30
msgid ""
"@ import sys\n"
"@ sys.path.insert(0, '')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:37
msgid "Inline scripting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:40
msgid "Inline import"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:42
msgid ""
"Use ``@.imp`` as an inline importer (xonsh >= 0.18.2) — modules are looked "
"up lazily without a preceding ``import`` statement:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:45
msgid ""
"@ @.imp.json.loads($(echo '{\"a\":1}'))\n"
"{'a': 1}\n"
"\n"
"@ @.imp.datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()\n"
"'2024-02-12T15:29:57.125696'\n"
"\n"
"@ @.imp.hashlib.md5(b'Hello world').hexdigest()\n"
"'3e25960a79dbc69b674cd4ec67a72c62'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:56
msgid "Autocompletion works too — e.g. ``@.imp.date<TAB>``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:60
msgid "Inline statements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:62
msgid ""
"Use ``$[...]`` (or ``$(...)``) to embed subprocess statements directly "
"inside Python expressions — loops, comprehensions, conditionals, and context "
"managers all compose naturally:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:66
msgid ""
"@ for i in range(1, 5): $[echo @(i)]\n"
"\n"
"@ [$[echo @(i)] for i in range(1, 5)]\n"
"\n"
"@ if $(which vim): $[echo vim]\n"
"\n"
"@ $[echo vim] if $(which vim) else $[echo vi]\n"
"\n"
"@ with @.env.swap(QWE=1): $[bash -c 'echo $QWE']\n"
"\n"
"@ $A=1 $B=2 bash -c 'echo $A $B'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:82
msgid "String tricks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:85
msgid "Triple quotes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:87
msgid ""
"To avoid escape characters (``echo \"\\\"hello\\\"\"``) and keep strings "
"readable, use triple quotes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:90
msgid ""
"@ echo \"\"\"{\"hello\":'world'}\"\"\"\n"
"{\"hello\":'world'}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:97
msgid "f-strings in commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:99
msgid ""
"f-strings work both inside ``@(...)`` and standalone as arguments (xonsh >= "
"0.23.0):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:102
msgid ""
"@ echo @(f'Hello {$HOME}')\n"
"Hello /home/snail\n"
"\n"
"@ echo f'Hello {$HOME}'\n"
"Hello /home/snail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:112
msgid "Walrus operator in action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:114
msgid ""
"Python's walrus operator ``:=`` (`PEP 572 <https://peps.python.org/pep-0572/"
">`_) lets you capture a value inline and reuse it later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:117
msgid "In subprocess:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:119
msgid ""
"@ echo Hello @(_name := input('Name: '))  # use ``_`` to keep the env clean\n"
"@ echo Hello again @(_name)\n"
"Name: Mike\n"
"Hello Mike\n"
"Hello again Mike"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:127
msgid ""
"Works with JSON/struct output too — decorators like ``@json`` return Python "
"objects which the walrus captures for reuse:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:130
msgid ""
"@ (servers := $(@json echo '[\"srv1\", \"srv2\"]'))\n"
"['srv1', 'srv2']\n"
"\n"
"@ echo @(servers[0])\n"
"srv1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:140
msgid "Shadowing between shell commands and Python names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:142
msgid ""
"Because xonsh parses Python-mode first, a bare name that resolves as a "
"Python built-in is evaluated as Python rather than run as a shell command. "
"Classic collisions: ``id``, ``zip``, ``dir``, ``import``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:146
msgid ""
"@ id\n"
"<built-in function id> # Python built-in, not /usr/bin/id"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:151
msgid "Workarounds:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:153
msgid ""
"**Flip the experimental toggle** `$XONSH_BUILTINS_TO_CMD <envvars."
"html#XONSH_BUILTINS_TO_CMD>`_ — when set, bare built-in names are run as "
"subprocess commands if a matching alias or executable exists, falling back "
"to the Python built-in otherwise. The same switch is also useful on Windows "
"for ``dir`` (see `platforms <platforms.html#name-space-conflicts>`_)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:159
msgid ""
"**Change the case**: ``Zip`` or ``ZIP`` — Python names are case-sensitive, "
"so ``Zip`` misses the built-in and falls through to a command lookup. On "
"case-insensitive filesystems (macOS default, Windows) ``Zip`` then resolves "
"to the ``zip`` executable. On case-sensitive filesystems (typical Linux) "
"this only works if such a name exists in ``$PATH`` — use a different "
"workaround below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:165
msgid ""
"**Force subprocess mode** with ``$[...]``, ``$(...)`` or a redirect: "
"``$[id]``, ``$(id)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:167
msgid "**Alias it under a different name**: ``aliases['ids'] = 'id'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:168
msgid "**Use `xontrib-abbrevs`** to auto-expand on space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../python.rst:171
msgid ""
"See also: `xonsh-cheatsheet <https://github.com/anki-code/xonsh-"
"cheatsheet>`_ for more copy-pastable examples."
msgstr ""
